  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "The ultimate goal of computer development is to make technology disappear from the user's point of view. Historically, computers were complex and required technical experts, but modern connected devices are moving toward this goal. These systems are interactive, meaning lay users can operate them without needing to understand the underlying technology.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "The evolution of Augmented Reality (AR) can be broadly divided into four phases, though these phases may sometimes overlap. These stages include the Experimental phase (before the 1980s), the Basic Research phase (1980s to mid-1990s), the Tools or Applications phase (mid-1990s to 2007), and the current Commercial Application phase starting in 2007.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "Reducing cyber sickness is extremely important because it greatly enhances the overall user experience of VR. A major challenge is that current mitigation strategies often reduce presence, which compromises the immersive experience. Therefore, the objective is to minimize sickness by reducing navigation duration or distance while simultaneously maximizing presence.",
  "explanations": []
}

{
  "recap": "This lecture introduces AR software, noting that numerous tools are available for developers. To provide a structured view, these tools will be organized into categories based on the programming skills required. The categories range from low-level software libraries, which demand considerable skill, to simple authoring tools designed for novice users.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "Navigation consists of two components: locomotion (travel) and wayfinding. Wayfinding is the process of building a cognitive map of the surroundings, which helps users locate themselves and find a path to a destination. This mental model relies on three types of spatial knowledge: landmark, procedural, and topological knowledge.",
