    ]
}

{
  "recap": "Because image generation (rendering) is a complex, computation-heavy process, relying solely on the CPU would prevent the computer from performing other tasks. To increase efficiency, rendering is handled by a dedicated component called the graphics card, which contains a GPU or graphics processing unit. The CPU assigns these rendering tasks to the display controller, allowing the GPU to perform the necessary computations without involving the CPU.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "Hardware design for visual displays must be informed by basic physiological properties, as engineered systems need to adequately fool our senses. Therefore, VR displays should be designed to match the performance of the visual sense, considering three key factors. These factors are spatial resolution, intensity resolution and range, and temporal resolution, which relates to frame rates and motion perception.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "Designing for universal usability requires accommodating three user classes: novice, intermittent, and expert. Systems should support all these categories, as demonstrated by offering both hotkeys and menu options in a word processor. This allows experts to use faster shortcuts, while novices and intermittent users can rely on recognition-based menu options.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "The system incorporates location-specific sounds, such as temple bells, and uses an interactive tablet as the main interface for navigation and information. This virtual tablet includes a miniature map of the island, a video player for drone footage, and various info elements. The design was inspired by real-world tablet usage to ensure familiarity and ease of use, adhering to established design guidelines.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "This lecture continues the discussion on visual perception by applying previous knowledge of vision to understand how head-mounted displays work. The segment then detailed the structure of the human eye, which includes the cornea (a powerful lens), the natural lens (with variable focus), and the retina. The retina serves as the non-planar image surface where light rays fall before being transmitted to the brain.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "The discussion shifted to the key concerns for building the Majuli VR system. Major issues include capturing both the tangible and intangible aspects of the island's cultural heritage, and ensuring a strong user experience, specifically the sense of presence. Finally, the team must also address and minimize the risk of cyber sickness for users exploring the virtual island.",
