  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "There are two primary ways to design an experiment when assigning tasks: within-subject and between-subjects. In a within-subject design, all participants perform tasks corresponding to all test conditions. Conversely, a between-subjects design involves dividing the tasks and distributing them among different subsets of participants.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "When light strikes a material surface, it interacts, and this interaction can result in three possible outcomes: transmission, absorption, or reflection. Transmission occurs when light passes through the material, while absorption happens when the light energy is taken in by the object. It is important to note that all three of these processes can occur simultaneously.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "The Virtual World Generator (VWG) is the abstraction responsible for creating a proper VR experience. It receives various inputs, such as head tracker data and controller inputs, which are queued for processing. The VWG's primary role is to maintain an internal reality, converting input information into output so that the user perceives a real experience.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "Colored light sources use three components (red, green, and blue) for both emitted intensity and reflection coefficients. To compute the final color, these three components must be calculated individually. When multiple colored light sources are present, the total intensity is determined by adding the ambient light to a summation of contributions from each individual light source.",
  "explanations": [
    ]
}

{
  "recap": "The focus of this lecture shifts from optimizing the VR experience, such as reducing cyber sickness, to optimizing the resource requirements of VR systems. Because extended reality is resource hungry, we must build systems that minimize resource usage. The primary objective is to minimize resource requirements while simultaneously maximizing the user experience.",
